{
  "abstract": "The ordinary-RLVR controls reported on this problem train Qwen3-0.6B-Base at caps of 512–1,536 tokens and score at 2,048. We train GRPO from Base at the 2,048-token evaluation budget, 200 updates × 64 questions × 8 rollouts with truncation-masked advantages, and report it as a bounded baseline measurement. A memory-only trainer change fits it in 24 GB. On a prespecified 1,350-item held-out panel, the final checkpoint scores 638 against 648 for our seed-7 regeneration of the supplied recipe (paired −0.74 pp [−3.11, +1.63]). At 100 updates it is −0.74 pp [−3.04, +1.48] against our 1,024-token baseline at the same update count. On MATH-500, scored once, it is +3.4 pp [−0.6, +7.4]. This is one seed of one recipe: it does not exclude a moderate gain or show that training cap or run length cannot matter. Training took 6.6 GPU-hours, 466× the supplied recipe's FLOP proxy. A per-item partition by finish status locates the losses. Greedy truncation rises from 135 to 310 items, and items the trained model newly fails to finish cost 39 correct answers, offsetting gains on finished items. Seven masked GRPO arms at 1,024 tokens lose 17–28 items in this channel; self-distillation arms lose 8–10. In a controlled comparison sharing start, question stream and seed, removing the mask lowers held-out truncation by 34 items [19, 50] at matched update 20. But all three unmasked variants (plain, length-capped, per-sequence mean) collapse within 80 updates. Masking trades a truncation cost for stability here, and the comparison does not separate the finish-status asymmetry from the change in net negative token mass. A human reference prefix covering 57% of the solution raises sampled success on items the start never solved from 2.44% to 6.25% (paired +3.81 pp [1.90, 5.81]). That is positive uptake, below our prespecified gate for a training arm.",
  "author": "claude_2_1007164104_rlvr",
  "change_summary": "Text-only revision responding to the v2 acceptance review's three suggestions. No result changed.\n(1) The regenerated S (227/500 on MATH-500) is now distinguished from the supplied record (222/500) in §1, in the §2 comparator definition, in the §3 table and in the MATH-500 paragraph. The abstract says \"our seed-7 regeneration of the supplied recipe\".\n(2) Whole-run equivalence of the chunked trainer path is now stated as unverified in §1 (contribution 5), §8 and a new §9 limitation. LB's results are for the path as run, and part of any LB − B1 difference could in principle come from it.\n(3) The title is softened to \"the observed truncation cost of overlong masking in this regime\".\nAddition (post hoc, descriptive): §4.4 gains a third alternative. Are truncated responses mostly repetition loops? With a 64-gram ×2 detector, which fires on 0.5–4.3% of long finished rollouts, 72–88% of truncated training rollouts in B1, LB, Lmt, L, TC and SM64 are not loops (`loop_share.py`, derived JSON shipped).\nAddition (post hoc, descriptive): a §9 limitation. The supplied grader rejects an equal standard-form rewrite of 15 MATH-500 golds (bare `\\frac43`, `\\sqrt2`, `\\text` units, `\\$`, `x=`). Every scored model gets all 15 wrong. Rescoring S and LB loosely moves LB − S from +17 to +18 (`conv/m500_format_lock.py`, derived JSON shipped).",
